<span class="ltx_personname">Hui Xue
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Alibaba Group
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">hui.xue@alibaba-inc.com</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xiting Wang
</span><span id="id5" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>˜˜Corresponding author.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Renmin University of China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">xitingwang@ruc.edu.cn</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite extensive alignment efforts, Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) remain vulnerable to jailbreak attacks, posing serious safety risks. To address this, existing detection methods either learn attack-specific parameters, which hinders generalization to unseen attacks, or rely on heuristically sound principles, which limit accuracy and efficiency. To overcome these limitations, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning to Detect (LoD)</span>, a general framework that accurately detects unknown jailbreak attacks by shifting the focus from attack-specific learning to task-specific learning. This framework includes a Multi-modal Safety Concept Activation Vector module for safety-oriented representation learning and a Safety Pattern Auto-Encoder module for unsupervised attack classification. Extensive experiments show that our method achieves consistently higher detection AUROC on diverse unknown attacks while improving efficiency. The code is available at <a href="https://anonymous.4open.science/r/Learning-to-Detect-51CB" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://anonymous.4open.science/r/Learning-to-Detect-51CB</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15430v1/pipeline4_v3.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/138;" width="476" height="138" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Comparison of attack detection methods and illustration of types of data used.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs), which empower Large Language Models (LLMs) to process modalities beyond text through various multimodal fusion approaches, have made rapid progress <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite>. However, the incorporation of visual modules brings substantial new safety challenges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Studies have shown that the additional visual inputs in LVLMs form a continuous, high-dimensional attack surface, where subtle adversarial perturbations in images can bypass safety filters undetectably <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
The seriousness of this vulnerability has been revealed by recent jailbreak attack methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024a)</a></cite>, which have already induced LVLMs to answer 96% of unsafe questions on AdvBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2022)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As a key remedy to the aforementioned safety vulnerability, attack detection has attracted increasing attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. (2025a)</a></cite>.
Despite its importance, developing a detection method that is both <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">accurate</span> and <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">generalizable</span> to diverse jailbreak attacks remains challenging (Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>):</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1em;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">learning-based</em> methods, such as LlamaGuard2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team (2024)</a></cite> and GradSafe-Adapt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2024)</a></cite>, achieve good accuracy mainly by learning attack-specific parameters, using training data from those attacks. There is no guarantee that the learned attack-specific parameters can well generalize to previously unseen attacks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-learning-based</em> methods like JailGuard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025)</a></cite>, HiddenDetect <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2025)</a></cite>, and GradSafe-Zero <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2024)</a></cite> operate mainly on heuristically sound principles rather than learned parameters. By eliminating reliance on attack-specific training data, these methods can be applicable to unseen attacks, even future ones that have not been developed. Nevertheless, their accuracy remains limited by the intrinsic constraints of heuristic-based methods: achieving high accuracy in complex or unforeseen scenarios typically requires an extensive set of rules or heuristics, which are difficult to comprehensively enumerate or anticipate in advance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">De Gasperis and Facchini (2025)</a></cite>. Moreover, these methods are typically less <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">efficient</em> than learning-based methods (see experimental results in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.5 Computational Efficiency Analysis ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address the aforementioned issues, we harness the advantages of learning for detecting <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unknown</em> attacks, proposing a framework for <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning to Detect (LoD) unknown jailbreak attacks<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">1</span></span>
              
              
              
            <span id="footnote1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">For brevity, we use the term “attack” to refer to “jailbreak attack” throughout the remainder of this paper.</span></span></span></span></span> This framework learns parameters that are tailored for the detection <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">task</em> rather than specific <em id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attacks</em>, achieving high accuracy, strong generalizability to unknown attacks, as well as good computation efficiency.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,
our training data includes only safe and unsafe questions that have not been modified by using any attack methods. The absence of attack-specific training data lays a foundation for good generalization.
To model the complexity of jailbreak detection, we learn two modules, representation learning and attack classification,
as shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and elaborated below:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">representation learning</em> module transforms an input prompt
into a vector that well quantifies safety risks in the input.
To achieve this goal, we propose <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-modal Safety Concept Activation Vectors (MSCAV)</span>, which estimate the probability that the LVLM considers an input as unsafe across all model layers. Notably, even without exposure to attack-modified inputs during training, these layer-wise fine-grained safety-related representations contain abundant information that can help distinguish attacks from safe prompts (Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.1 MSCAV Classifiers ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attack classification</em> module detects attacks based on the MSCAVs generated by the representation learning module. Directly aggregating each feature in the MSCAV, e.g., by using max pooling, fails to model cross-layer interactions of safety patterns, which have been observed in large models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024b)</a></cite>. We solve this issue by <em id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">learning inter-layer dependencies</em>, enabling a more flexible, data-driven modeling of safety patterns that boosts accuracy. To simultaneously ensure generalizability, we minimize assumptions about attacks and train this module exclusively on <em id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">safe</em> inputs. The key for training with only safe inputs is to formulate the task as anomaly detection: common patterns of safe inputs are modeled during training, and deviations from safety patterns during testing indicate anomalies, i.e., unsafe or attacked inputs. To this end, we introduce a <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Safety Pattern Auto-Encoder</span> that reconstructs MSCAVs of safe inputs and yields low reconstruction error for in-distribution safe inputs and high error for attacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To summarize, our contributions include:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1em;">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning to Detect (LoD) framework</span>: we propose a general framework that detects unknown attacks accurately and efficiently by learning parameters for the detection task rather than for specific attacks. While we focus on jailbreak attacks against LVLMs, the LoD framework is broadly applicable to other modalities and attack forms.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MSCAV and Safety Pattern Auto-Encoder</span>: we introduce two learning modules for safety-oriented representation learning and attack classification, which enable accurate and efficient detection of unseen jailbreak attacks without relying on attack-specific training data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Extensive experiments</span> on three LVLMs and five benchmarks show that our method improves the minimum AUROC across five diverse attacks by at most 62.31% and improves the computation efficiency by up to 62.7%.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15430v1/pipeline3_v2.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:999/454;" width="999" height="454" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overview of our Learning to Detect (LoD) framework, which consists of (a) a representation learning module and (b) an attack classification module. In our framework, attacked inputs are only seen during testing. During training, the representation learning module is trained on safe and unsafe inputs that are not attacked to extract safety-related representations (MSCAVs). The Safety Pattern Auto-Encoder is then trained exclusively on MSCAVs of safe inputs to model their typical distribution.
During testing, the Safety Pattern Auto-Encoder reconstructs input MSCAVs. Attacked inputs, whose patterns differ from the learned typical distribution of safe MSCAVs, produce high reconstruction errors, while safe inputs yield low reconstruction errors.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an input prompt <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> for an LVLM <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, our goal is to decide whether <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is a safe input or an attacked one in an accurate, generalizable, and efficient manner. To achieve this, we propose a Learning To Detect (LoD) framework. As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, LoD consists of two major modules designed to learn parameters essential for the attack detection task rather than for specific attack types. The first module, the <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MSCAV classifiers</span>, learns a representation for <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> that well quantifies its safety risks, using only safe and unsafe inputs that are not attacked as training data.
The second module, the <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Safety Pattern Auto-Encoder</span>, improves detection accuracy by modeling inter-layer dependencies among safe patterns by reconstructing the MSCAVs of safe inputs. <em id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Attacked inputs are not seen until testing.</em></p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>MSCAV Classifiers</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To facilitate subsequent attack classification, we first learn a representation of input <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> that is useful for distinguishing whether <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is safe or not.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Existing methods and our improvement</span>.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2025)</a></cite> have shown that the internal activations of an LVLM <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> inherently encode safety-relevant signals. Their method, HiddenDetect, achieved state-of-the-art detection accuracy by representing an input <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> through these activations. We further improve detection performance over HiddenDetect by <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">filtering out detection-irrelevant information</em> from the activations while preserving safety-related signals. The importance of focusing on safety-critical parameters has also been validated by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2024)</a></cite>, whose GradSafe method selects safety-critical parameters to improve detection accuracy. Unlike GradSafe, our method (1) assigns a <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">soft weight</em> to each parameter to reflect its relative contribution to safety modeling, rather than performing hard selection, and (2) operates directly on internal activations, which, compared with gradients that are used in GradSafe, are both <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">more efficient to compute</em> and empirically <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">more effective</em> for attack detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The basic idea</span>. Our idea is to extract safety-related information from the internal activations of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> based on existing model interpretation methods. We base our method on the Safety Concept Activation Vector (SCAV) method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, due to its verified effectiveness in interpreting the safety mechanisms of large language models (LLMs), and extend it into a multi-modal version.
Given the internal activations <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}^{l}\in\mathbf{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}^{l}\in\mathbf{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> at layer <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, this interpretation method outputs the probability that a model considers the input as unsafe at this layer.
We observe that concatenating these unsafe probabilities at different layers form a vector (i.e., our MSCAV) that retains only safety-related information while filtering out safety-irrelevant signals, making such vectors suitable for safety-related tasks such as attack detection; 2) illustrate that MSCAVs contain sufficient information for detecting attacks even if they are trained without seeing attacked inputs; and 3) show that SCAV can be accurately applied to interpret safety mechanisms of LVLMs, despite the fact that it is developed and verified only for LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MSCAV computation</span>.
We first introduce how to construct the initial MSCAV <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{\text{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{\text{o}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then discuss how this can be refined into the final representation <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{\text{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mtext>r</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{\text{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which ensures better accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Constructing the initial MSCAV <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{\text{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">o</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{\text{o}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</em>
Given the internal activations <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{e}^{1},...,\mathbf{e}^{L}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{e}^{1},...,\mathbf{e}^{L}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of layers in the LVLM, we train <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> classifiers, <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{C_{1},\ldots,C_{L}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{C_{1},\ldots,C_{L}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, to convert the internal activations into the initial MSCAV <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{\text{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{\text{o}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{\text{o}}=\big[C_{1}(\mathbf{e}^{1}),C_{2}(\mathbf{e}^{2}),\dots,C_{L}(\mathbf{e}^{L})\big]^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{\text{o}}=\big[C_{1}(\mathbf{e}^{1}),C_{2}(\mathbf{e}^{2}),\dots,C_{L}(\mathbf{e}^{L})\big]^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{l}(\mathbf{e}^{l})\in\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{l}(\mathbf{e}^{l})\in\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the probability that the LVLM considers the input <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> as unsafe at layer <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{\text{o}}\in\mathbf{R}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{\text{o}}\in\mathbf{R}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is thus a vector that filters out safety-irrelevant signals in the internal activations and retains only layer-wise safety information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The key question here is how to learn classifiers <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{C_{1},\ldots,C_{L}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{C_{1},\ldots,C_{L}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. SCAV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024b)</a></cite> illustrates that <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{C_{1},\ldots,C_{L}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{C_{1},\ldots,C_{L}\}</annotation></semantics></math> can take the simple form of linear classifiers while still yielding accurate results under the <em id="S2.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">linear separability assumption</em>, meaning that the activations of safe and unsafe inputs are linearly separable at layer <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>. We validate this assumption in aligned LVLMs at the end of this subsection and
in Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.1 MSCAV Classifiers ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
Under this assumption, we can model <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> as a linear classifier:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{l}(\mathbf{e}^{l})=\mathrm{sigmoid}(\mathbf{w}^{\top}\mathbf{e}^{l}+b)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sigmoid</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{l}(\mathbf{e}^{l})=\mathrm{sigmoid}(\mathbf{w}^{\top}\mathbf{e}^{l}+b),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}\in\mathbf{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}\in\mathbf{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> are parameters to learn, and each element <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}\in\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}\in\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the importance of the <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th hidden dimension in extracting safety-related information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The classifiers are trained to distinguish safe inputs from unsafe ones based on internal activations. The training data consists of multi-modal safe inputs <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, in which both text and image content are safe, and multi-modal unsafe inputs <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, in which the text includes unsafe content and images depict a matching scene (see Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b) for an example). Formally, each classifier <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained by using a binary cross-entropy loss:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{C_{l}}=-\frac{1}{|N|}\sum\big[&amp;y\log C_{l}(\mathbf{e}^{l})\\
&amp;+(1-y)\log(1-C_{l}(\mathbf{e}^{l}))\big],\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{C_{l}}=-\frac{1}{|N|}\sum\big[&amp;y\log C_{l}(\mathbf{e}^{l})\\
&amp;+(1-y)\log(1-C_{l}(\mathbf{e}^{l}))\big],\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> are labels indicating whether the inputs are safe (0) or unsafe (1), and <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the training data size.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Generating the refined MSCAV <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{\text{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">r</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{\text{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</em>
To further remove noise, we refine <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{\text{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{\text{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> by retaining only the layers where the classifiers achieve test accuracy above the threshold <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.1 MSCAV Classifiers ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, that is, <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{s}=\{l\mid\mathcal{P}_{l}\geq P_{0}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>l</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{s}=\{l\mid\mathcal{P}_{l}\geq P_{0}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The refined MSCAV <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{\text{r}}\in\mathbf{R}^{|\mathcal{L}_{s}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mtext>r</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{\text{r}}\in\mathbf{R}^{|\mathcal{L}_{s}|}</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed as:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{\text{r}}=\big[C_{l}(\mathbf{e}^{l})\big]_{l\in\mathcal{L}_{s}}^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mtext>r</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{\text{r}}=\big[C_{l}(\mathbf{e}^{l})\big]_{l\in\mathcal{L}_{s}}^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.15430v1/pattern1.svg" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/314;" width="476" height="314"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>The average MSCAV of safe inputs, unsafe inputs, and inputs attacked by unknown jailbreak methods (marked in brackets). MSCAVs possess the discriminative power for distinguishing attacks from safe inputs.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S2.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.15430v1/linear_separability.svg" id="S2.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/317;" width="476" height="317"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Test accuracy of MSCAV classifiers across layers in LVLMs. High accuracy indicates safe and unsafe inputs are linearly at the corresponding layer.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effectiveness of MSCAVs in distinguishing attacks.</span>
As shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.1 MSCAV Classifiers ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, even though the MSCAV classifiers are trained without seeing attacked inputs or their labels, the resulting MSCAVs inherently capture sufficient information that help differentiate attacks from safe inputs:
the curves of attacked inputs remain clearly above the safe ones, although they generally fall between those of unsafe and safe inputs. This pattern suggests that attacks reduce the unsafe probability across layers yet preserve distinguishable traces from safe inputs. This finding indicates that these attacks, which are primarily optimized for cheating the LVLMs at the <em id="S2.SS1.p9.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">output layer</em>, leave traces of unsafe inputs in the <em id="S2.SS1.p9.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">intermediate layers</em>, which can easily be identified by using layer-wise MSCAVs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verifying linear separability in LVLMs</span>.
We evaluate the linear separability of inputs in LVLMs by training classifiers in each layer and measuring their accuracy in distinguishing safe inputs (<math id="S2.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>) from unsafe ones (<math id="S2.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>).
As shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.1 MSCAV Classifiers ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, classifiers achieve consistently high test accuracy across layers, indicating that the safe and unsafe inputs are linearly separable in the activation space, verifying the linear separability assumption. Remarkably, the accuracy exceeds 90% as early as the 4th layer, much earlier than the 10th layer typically observed in LLMs. This result suggests that the additional visual information enables the safety concept to emerge in earlier layers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Safety Pattern Auto-Encoder</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This module aims to detect unknown attacks accurately based on the MSCAVs. Directly selecting dimension-wise thresholds on the MSCAVs ignores inter-layer dependencies, which are critical to safety patterns modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, and thus limits accuracy (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Ablation Study ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>). To overcome this, we propose learning <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inter-layer dependencies</em> in a flexible, data-driven manner, while ensuring generalizability by training exclusively on <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">safe</em> inputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Formulation as anomaly detection.</span> To train using only safe inputs, we formulate the task as anomaly detection. During training, the module learns common patterns in safe inputs. During testing, deviations from the learned safety pattern indicate attacks or unsafe inputs.
To this end, we introduce the Safety Pattern Auto-Encoder (SPAE), which is trained to reconstruct MSCAVs of safe inputs.
During testing, a low reconstruction error indicates in-distribution safe inputs, while a high reconstruction error indicates possible attacks or unsafe inputs, following existing works in anomaly detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sakurada and Yairi (2014)</a></cite>. We next describe the architecture of SPAE, as well as its training and testing strategies in detail.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SPAE architecture.</span> SPAE uses a standard auto-encoder with a three-layer fully connected encoder and a symmetric decoder with ReLU activations. This hierarchical non-linear structure enables the model to capture complex inter-layer safety relations, considering that each dimension in SCAVs correspond to one layer. Formally, the encoder <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{enc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>enc</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{enc}}</annotation></semantics></math> compresses the MSCAV <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}\text{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>r</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}\text{r}</annotation></semantics></math> into a low-dimensional latent space, and the decoder <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{dec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>dec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{dec}}</annotation></semantics></math> reconstructs it back to the original dimensionality <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{L}\text{s}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{L}\text{s}|</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{S}}\text{r}=f_{\text{dec}}\big(f_{\text{enc}}(\mathbf{S}\text{r})\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐒</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>r</mtext></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>dec</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>enc</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>r</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{S}}\text{r}=f_{\text{dec}}\big(f_{\text{enc}}(\mathbf{S}\text{r})\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SPAE training.</span>
The model is trained solely on safe inputs to minimize assumptions about attacks. The training objective minimizes the mean squared reconstruction error between the input and reconstructed MSCAVs:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}=\frac{1}{N_{s}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{s}}||\hat{\mathbf{S}}\text{r}-\mathbf{S}\text{r}||_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></munderover><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐒</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>r</mtext></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}=\frac{1}{N_{s}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{s}}||\hat{\mathbf{S}}\text{r}-\mathbf{S}\text{r}||_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of safe inputs. This loss encourages the auto-encoder to learn a compact representation of safety patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SPAE testing.</span> During testing, SPAE detects anomalies by evaluating the reconstruction error:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=||\hat{\mathbf{S}}\text{r}-\mathbf{S}\text{r}||_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐒</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>r</mtext></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=||\hat{\mathbf{S}}\text{r}-\mathbf{S}\text{r}||_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Given an input (safe or attacked), if <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds a threshold <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, the input is flagged as attacked. Otherwise, the input is considered safe. Threshold selection is detailed in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Threshold Decision ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Evaluation Using Additional Criteria ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Advantages over prior methods.</span> Unlike approaches that rely solely on cosine similarity over raw LVLM embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2025)</a></cite> or selected gradients <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2024)</a></cite>, which assume feature independence and equal contribution of features, SPAE explicitly learns inter-feature dependencies of safety patterns. This design eliminates strong independence assumptions and substantially improves detection accuracy (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Overall Detection Performance ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
