<td id="S3.T1.2.1.23.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">+2.36%</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.23.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">+3.34%</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.23.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">+12.86%</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.23.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">+6.21%</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparison of detection AUROC across different LVLMs and attack methods.
Methods are categorized into two categories based on whether they utilize only the input and output of LVLMs<sup id="S3.T1.7" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S3.T1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a)</span></sup> or utilize information of internal activations or gradients in the LVLMs<sup id="S3.T1.8" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S3.T1.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b)</span></sup>. The best results are shown in <span id="S3.T1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>, and the second-best results are <span id="S3.T1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span>. <math id="S3.T1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the relative improvement of our LoD method over the best baseline.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experimental Setups</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jailbreak attack methods.</span> To evaluate the performance of our detection method, we select representative attack approaches along with their corresponding datasets. For <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prompt-manipulation-based</em> attacks, we use: <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MML</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">JOOD</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong et al. (2025)</a></cite>, and <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HADES</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite>. For <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">adversarial-perturbation-based</em> attacks, we employ <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VAJM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al. (2024)</a></cite> and <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UMK</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024a)</a></cite>. Details of these attack methods are introduced in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Jailbreak Attacks ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training and validation datasets.</span>
To train LoD and select hyperparameters, we use unsafe inputs <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> from AdvBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2022)</a></cite>, which contains prompts describing unsafe behaviors. Safe inputs <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> are from GQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hudson and Manning (2019)</a></cite>, a large-scale visual reasoning dataset for compositional question answering. We combine prompts from both datasets and create multimodal inputs by synthesizing images for both unsafe and safe texts using Pixart-Sigma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Among the resulting safe-unsafe pairs, 100 are used for training the MSCAV classifiers, and 100 pairs are used for validation. The autoencoder is trained on 320 safe inputs, reserving 80 for validation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Testing datasets.</span>
We use two sets of testing data. The <em id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">first set</em> includes 400 safe inputs from SEED-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite> and 400 unsafe inputs from MM-SafetyBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024c)</a></cite>. The attacked inputs are created by applying the attack methods on the unsafe inputs, except for HADES, which provides its own dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite> that we directly use.
In the <em id="S3.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">second set</em>, the safe inputs are replaced by 400 samples from MM-Vet2. We report the results of the first set in the main paper and the results of the second set in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Supplementary Evaluation with MM-Vet2 ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline.</span>
To ensure a comprehensive evaluation and highlight the strengths of our proposed method, we select representative baselines from two main categories of detection approaches:
(a) For methods that do not use internal representations, we include CIDER <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024a)</a></cite>, MirrorCheck <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fares et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and JailGuard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025)</a></cite>;
(b) For methods leveraging internal representations, we incorporate HiddenDetect <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2025)</a></cite> and GradSafe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2024)</a></cite>. For GradSafe, we use GradSafe-Zero instead of GradSafe-Adapt, as the latter requires domain adaptation for jailbreak inputs, whereas our goal is to detect attacks without jailbreak labels. Results for GradSafe-Adapt are reported in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Results on GradSafe-Adapt ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Evaluation Using Additional Criteria ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>, showing limited generalization to unseen attacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation criteria.</span>
We follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2025)</a></cite> and evaluate detection accuracy using <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AUROC</span>, which reflects performance across all decision thresholds. Results on additional criteria, including <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F1</span> and TPR at low FPR (e.g., FPR
<math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math> 0.01), are reported in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Supplementary Evaluation Using Additional Criteria ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. We also report the computation time in <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">seconds</span> to analyze efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LVLMs.</span>
We evaluate our method on three LVLMs: LLaVA-1.6-vicuna-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, CogVLM-chat-v1.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2023)</a></cite>, and Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hyperparameter settings.</span>
We set the hyperparameters as follows for all experiments. For <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we choose 0.9. Although AUROC is independent of the specific threshold, we set the thresholds for the three LVLMs as described in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Threshold Decision ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Evaluation Using Additional Criteria ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>. The settings for the baselines are given in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Settings for Baseline Methods ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Overall Detection Performance</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the AUROC scores of various detection methods across three LVLMs and five jailbreak attack methods. Here, we conduct the experiments on the first set of testing data (Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Experimental Setups ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>). The results on the second set of testing data are given in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Supplementary Evaluation with MM-Vet2 ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. Based on the results, we have two major observations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, our proposed LoD consistently achieves the highest performance, outperforming the strongest baselines with relative improvements ranging from <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.91\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.91</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.91\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15.04\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>15.04</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15.04\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and substantially improving worst-case performance (i.e., minimum AUROC across attacks) with gains up to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="62.31\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>62.31</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">62.31\%</annotation></semantics></math> on LLaVA, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10.73\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10.73</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10.73\%</annotation></semantics></math> on Qwen2.5-VL, and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.86\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.86</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.86\%</annotation></semantics></math> on CogVLM, demonstrating strong detection performance across diverse unseen attacks. We analyze why our method achieves a high AUROC of around 0.999 in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Reconstruction Error Statistics ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, baselines that leverage internal representations (GradSafe and HiddenDetect), generally achieve higher average AUROC than non-internal methods (MirrorCheck, CIDER, and JailGuard), reflecting the benefits of utilizing internal representations. However, they do not always achieve better minimum AUROC (see the <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Min</span> column), indicating that their heuristic principles may not be universally effective across all attacks and LVLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To better understand the contribution of each core component in the LoD framework, we
compare our method with the following ablated variants:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1em;">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoD w/o MSCAV</span>: This variant assesses the effectiveness of the representation learning module by replacing MSCAVs with raw internal activations concatenated across all layers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoD w/o SPAE</span>: This variant demonstrates the effectiveness of SPAE by replacing it with a simple aggregation of MSCAV features: an input is flagged as unsafe if any dimension of the refined MSCAV <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{\text{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mtext>r</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{\text{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds its corresponding threshold selected using the validation set (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Threshold Decision ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Evaluation Using Additional Criteria ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoD w/ HiddenDetect</span>: This variant compares our MSCAV-based representation learning with that in an existing state-of-the-art method, HiddenDetect. Specifically, we replace our MSCAVs with the feature extraction method proposed in HiddenDetect <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.15430v1/ablation_results.svg" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/371;" width="476" height="371"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>
Ablation study results showing the average AUROC scores on three LVLMs across five attack methods under different ablation settings. Both MSCAV and SPAE contribute to the detection accuracy. </figcaption>
</figure>
