<td id="S3.T2.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;">0.9999</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;">0.99</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;">0.9993</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;">0.9999</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;">0.9998</td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Average AUROC scores over five attack methods for different <math id="S3.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> thresholds across three LVLMs. The threshold we used is highlighted in gray. An analysis of why our method achieves a high AUROC of approximately 0.999 is provided in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Reconstruction Error Statistics ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The ablation study results in Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3.3 Ablation Study ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> highlight the necessity of both MSCAV and SPAE in the LoD framework.
Removing the MSCAV module (w/o MSCAV) leads to the most severe degradation across all three LVLMs (AUROC from <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.151" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.151</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.151</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.483" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.483</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.483</annotation></semantics></math>), underscoring its critical role in extracting discriminative safety-relevant features and mitigating redundancy in raw activations. When replacing MSCAV with HiddenDetect features (w/ HiddenDetect), performance improves relative to the ablated variants but remains notably below the full LoD model (AUROC from <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.629" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.629</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.629</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.813" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.813</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.813</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating that LoD’s MSCAV better generalizes safety signals across diverse LVLMs. Similarly, removing SPAE (w/o SPAE) also causes consistent drops (AUROC from <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.710" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.710</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.710</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.879" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.879</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.879</annotation></semantics></math>), demonstrating that modeling inter-layer interactions by using SPAE is essential for improving detection performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Parameter Sensitivity Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method involves two hyperparameters: the layer selection threshold <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the decision threshold <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because AUROC is computed by integrating over all possible <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> values, it is not affected by choices of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.
We therefore focus on analyzing the impact of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> here, while the effect of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> on threshold-dependent criteria (e.g., F1-score) is discussed in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Threshold Decision ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Evaluation Using Additional Criteria ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.3 Ablation Study ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that the average AUROC remains consistently high for all LVLMs and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> values, indicating that LoD is highly robust to the layer selection threshold. Moderate <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> values (e.g., 0.90) yield uniformly strong results, while overly large <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., 0.99) may cause minor degradation on LLaVA and Cogvlm due to the removal of informative layers. These results confirm that LoD maintains stable detection performance over a wide hyperparameter range.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Computational Efficiency Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.5 Computational Efficiency Analysis ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, our LoD method is the most efficient across three LVLMs, with a computation time ranging from 0.25 to 0.62 seconds. Here, most methods are run on a single NVIDIA A800 GPU, except for GradSafe on Qwen2.5-VL, which is run on 4 GPUs due to high memory usage.
As shown in the table, LoD reduces detection time by up to 62.7% compared to the best baseline on LLaVA, and shows improvements of 21.0% and 12.8% on Qwen2.5-VL and CogVLM, respectively.
In terms of training cost, fitting our MSCAV classifiers and the SPAE module requires only 12 and 2 seconds, respectively, which is a one-time offline training. These results highlight the efficiency of LoD.</p>
</div>
