</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detection methods.</span>
Existing detection methods can be categorized into two groups. Learning-based methods are typically trained on specific attacked data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alon and Kamfonas (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pi et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Although they achieve high accuracy on known attacks, their ability to generalize to unseen attacks is not guaranteed.
In contrast, non-learning-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fares et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2025)</a></cite> remove the dependence on attack-specific training data by relying on heuristic principles and can be applied to unseen attacks. However, their performance remains limited in complex or unforeseen scenarios.
Unlike these methods, our approach trains a learnable framework on both safe and unsafe inputs without attack-specific data, ensuring strong generalization and high detection performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jailbreak attacks.</span>
The multimodal nature of LVLMs creates new attack surfaces, especially in the visual modality, increasing vulnerability to jailbreaks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024d)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024a)</a></cite>. Existing attacks fall into two categories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024a)</a></cite>: prompt manipulation and adversarial perturbation. Prompt manipulation alters textual or visual inputs to bypass safety filters, usually without gradients <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024c)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite>, while adversarial perturbation uses gradient-based changes to induce unsafe outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Niu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shayegani et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Although existing detection approaches often fail to generalize or maintain satisfactory performance across different attacks, our learnable framework can accurately detect unseen jailbreaks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce Learning to Detect, a learnable framework that leverages LVLMs’ internal activations for jailbreak detection. While existing methods often struggle to achieve both high accuracy and generalization, LoD solves this by training task-specific parameters on safe and unsafe data, without relying on attack-specific data. Experiments show that LoD consistently outperforms state-of-the-art baselines across multiple LVLMs and previously unknown attacks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although our method achieves consistent performance across models and attacks, it has several limitations.
First, its results depend on the specific LVLM used.
Major architectural changes or new safety alignment mechanisms may require adapting the detector, though the prevalence of deep neural architectures suggests our approach will remain broadly applicable. Second, while our method is demonstrated to be effective against diverse jailbreaks, our evaluation cannot fully anticipate future adaptive attacks that specifically manipulate internal representations.
Exploring such scenarios is an important step toward building more resilient defenses.</p>
</div>
</section>
