<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Comparison of detection AUROC across different LVLMs and attack methods. The experiment is conducted on the second test set, in which the safe inputs are from MM-Vet2.
Methods are categorized into two categories based on whether they utilize only the input and output information of LVLMs<sup id="A1.T4.7" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A1.T4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a)</span></sup> or utilize information of internal representations in the LVLMs<sup id="A1.T4.8" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A1.T4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b)</span></sup>. The best results are shown in <span id="A1.T4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>, and the second-best results are <span id="A1.T4.10" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span>. <math id="A1.T4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the relative improvement of our LoD method over the best baseline.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide supplementary evaluation results using the second set of testing data, in which the safe inputs are from MM-Vet2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2024)</a></cite>. This further validates the accuracy and generalization of our proposed LoD method.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix A Supplementary Evaluation with MM-Vet2 ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, across all three models, LoD maintains high accuracy and strong generalization when evaluated with MM-Vet2 as the safe test dataset.
Specifically, LoD achieves the highest average AUROC of 0.9798 on LLaVA, 0.9820 on Qwen2.5-VL, and 0.9677 on CogVLM, outperforming the strongest baselines by 21.49%, 4.51%, and 18.07%, respectively.
It also obtains the best minimum AUROC in most cases, indicating enhanced generalization against diverse and challenging attacks.
While GradSafe slightly outperforms LoD on a few individual attack types (e.g., MML on LLaVA), LoD still delivers the best overall detection performance across models and attacks.
These results further confirm that LoD achieves both high accuracy and strong generalization without relying on attack-specific data. Furthermore, we analyze why we stably obtain high AUROC scores in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Reconstruction Error Statistics ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Supplementary Evaluation Using Additional Criteria</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further validate the effectiveness and robustness of our detection method, we note that the main text already demonstrates high AUROC scores. However, AUROC is an average criterion that may not fully reflect performance under specific operating conditions. In this section, we pursue two objectives: first, we measure TPR at low FPR to evaluate detection capability under strict false positive constraints; second, we determine practical thresholds for our method and verify its effectiveness at these thresholds.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
