<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>TPR at FPR=0.01 across different LVLMs and detection methods. Methods are categorized into two categories based on whether they utilize only the input and output information of LVLMs<sup id="A2.T5.8" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A2.T5.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a)</span></sup> or utilize information of internal representations in the LVLMs<sup id="A2.T5.9" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A2.T5.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b)</span></sup>. The safe-test data
are sampled from <span id="A2.T5.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SEED-Bench</span>. The best results are shown in <span id="A2.T5.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>, and the second-best results are shown in <span id="A2.T5.12" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underline</span>. <math id="A2.T5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the relative improvement of our method over the best baseline.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ B.1 TPR at Low FPR ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Evaluation Using Additional Criteria ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reports the TPR at a fixed FPR of 0.01. Most baseline methods exhibit very low TPR, often close to zero. This is because such a stringent FPR threshold only accepts the most confident predictions as positives; many jailbreak inputs are scored too similarly to safe inputs, causing the detectors to miss almost all attacks. GradSafe and HiddenDetect achieve moderate TPRs, suggesting that internal-representation-based methods can capture certain attack representations, but their effectiveness is highly sensitive to both the model and the jailbreak methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, our method consistently achieves competitive TPR across all attacks and LVLMs, reliably detecting true positives even under this strict operating point. These results highlight the accuracy and generalization of LoD, demonstrating its ability to generalize across diverse jailbreak strategies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Threshold Decision</h3>

<figure id="A2.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.15430v1/th.svg" id="A2.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:952/288;" width="952" height="288"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>F1 score sensitivity of different detection methods on the three models across threshold percentiles from 10th to 99th. Our method consistently achieves the highest F1 at each percentile, demonstrating robustness to threshold selection.</figcaption>
</figure>
