<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>F1 scores across different models and methods. Methods are categorized into two categories based on whether they utilize only the input and output information of LVLMs<sup id="A2.T6.8" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A2.T6.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a)</span></sup> or utilize information of internal representations in the LVLMs<sup id="A2.T6.9" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A2.T6.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b)</span></sup>. The safe-test data
are sampled from <span id="A2.T6.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SEED-Bench</span>. Best results among baselines are shown in <span id="A2.T6.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>, second-best baseline results are shown in <span id="A2.T6.12" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underline</span>. <math id="A2.T6.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the relative improvement of our method over the best baseline.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the effect of the SPAE, we set the threshold for each dimension as the maximum value of that MSCAV dimension on the auto-encoder’s validation set of safe inputs, with the results shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3.3 Ablation Study ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To determine thresholds for applying our method in real-world scenarios, we use the MOAT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. (2025b)</a></cite> dataset, which contains approximately 200 items, each consisting of a question for VLMs paired with a corresponding image.
MOAT serves as a collection of safe samples, which we use to align the measurement scores and select the 90th percentile as the threshold, thereby reducing the false positive rate. The resulting thresholds are 0.07502 for LLaVA, 0.07148 for Qwen2.5-VL, and 0.04083 for CogVLM. We apply the same procedure to determine thresholds for all baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the effectiveness of the chosen threshold, we report F1 scores across models and datasets in Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ B.1 TPR at Low FPR ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Evaluation Using Additional Criteria ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Our method consistently achieves high values, significantly outperforming all baselines. For Vicuna, we improve over the best baseline by +42.1% on average, with especially large gains on UMK (+46.8%). Similar improvements hold for Qwen2.5-VL (+7.7% on average) and CogVLM (+57.7%), indicating that the gains are not tied to a single model but are robust across architectures.
Notably, while baselines such as GradSafe or HiddenDetect achieve strong performance on certain datasets but collapse on others (often dropping to near zero), our method maintains consistently high F1 across all settings.
Such inconsistencies suggest that thresholds determined by these baselines lack generalizability: their score measurements for different safe samples vary considerably, leading to unstable and limited detection performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By contrast, our method maintains consistently high F1 across all settings, without significant degradation on any benchmark. This demonstrates not only that the threshold selected using MOAT generalizes well across different models but also that it remains effective under diverse attack scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sensitivity analysis.</span> To evaluate the impact of percentile selection on detection performance in practice, we investigate how the average F1 score changes from the 10th to the 99th percentile. Figure <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ B.2 Threshold Decision ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Evaluation Using Additional Criteria ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> presents the results for different methods and baselines. While the F1 scores of all methods fluctuate to some extent as the percentile varies, our method consistently achieves the highest detection performance with the smallest overall variation, demonstrating minimal sensitivity to percentile selection. This indicates that in practical scenarios, our detection can generalize against different attacks regardless of the chosen percentile. Furthermore, we analyze in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Reconstruction Error Statistics ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> why the average F1 score steadily increases and remains nearly constant as the percentile threshold rises.</p>
</div>
</section>
