</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Comparison of detection AUROC between LoD and GradSafe-Adapt across different models and datasets. The safe-test data
are sampled from <span id="A2.T7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SEED-Bench</span>.
The best results are shown in <span id="A2.T7.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>. <math id="A2.T7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the relative improvement of our LoD method over the GradSafe-Adapt.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we further compare GradSafe-Adapt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2024)</a></cite> with our LoD method. Following their original setting, we conduct domain adaptation using the official training split of the ToxicChat <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2023)</a></cite> benchmark.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#A2.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ B.3 Results on GradSafe-Adapt ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Evaluation Using Additional Criteria ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, while GradSafe-Adapt achieves reasonable detection performance on certain attack types, its effectiveness drops on some previously unseen jailbreak attacks (e.g., UMK for LLaVA and JOOD for CogVLM), leading to large performance gaps in both minimum and average AUROC. In contrast, our LoD method maintains consistently high performance across all attack types and models, demonstrating strong generalization to unseen jailbreak attacks without relying on attack-specific labels.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
