</span></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>Reconstruction error statistics (min, max, average, std) across different datasets and attacks for Vicuna, Qwen2.5-VL, and CogVLM. The <span id="A3.T8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Type</span> indicates the dataset’s usage: validation data is used for threshold selection (Sec. <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Threshold Decision ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Evaluation Using Additional Criteria ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>), while safe-test and attacked-test datasets are used to evaluate the performance of our method and baselines. The evaluation results based on SEED-Bench are shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, and the results based on MM-Vet2 are shown in Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix A Supplementary Evaluation with MM-Vet2 ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we analyze the reason for achieving a high AUROC of approximately 0.999 by investigating the reconstruction errors of the safe and attacked inputs. If the reconstruction errors of the safe inputs seldom overlap with those of the attacked ones in the testing data, it confirms that our method well separates these two groups of inputs and thus can achieve an AUROC that is close to 1. Our analysis results, shown in Table <a href="#A3.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix C Reconstruction Error Statistics ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, confirm this assumption.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">More specifically, we present the statistical features of reconstruction errors across different datasets: the threshold selection dataset MOAT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. (2025b)</a></cite>, the safe test sets SEED-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite> and MM-Vet2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, the attack dataset HADES <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite>, and the four remaining jailbreak attack datasets constructed from perturbations in MM-SafetyBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024c)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#A3.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix C Reconstruction Error Statistics ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we report the reconstruction error statistics (min, max, average, and std) for LLaVA, Qwen2.5-VL, and CogVLM across different datasets and attack types.
We observe that the AUROC scores reported in the main experiments (Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) are consistently very high.
This is largely because the maximum reconstruction errors of SEED-Bench samples are generally lower than the minimum reconstruction errors of many attack datasets, providing a clear separation for detection.
For MM-Vet2 (Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix A Supplementary Evaluation with MM-Vet2 ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), although the reconstruction errors are slightly higher than those on SEED-Bench, the mean errors remain substantially smaller than those on attack datasets.
Moreover, the moderately larger standard deviations indicate that most reconstruction errors are still below the mean and smaller than the minimum attack errors, which continues to support high AUROC values.
In threshold selection experiments, Figure <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ B.2 Threshold Decision ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Evaluation Using Additional Criteria ‣ Learning to Detect Unknown Jailbreak Attacksin Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows that our F1 score steadily increases with the chosen quantile, unlike other baselines that first increase and then decrease.
This is because the reconstruction errors in MOAT, used for threshold selection, are consistently smaller than those of SEED-Bench and attack datasets, ensuring that attack inputs are always correctly identified while safe samples are progressively more accurately classified as the threshold increases.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Jailbreak Attacks</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce the jailbreak attacks used in our experiments. To evaluate the performance of our detection method, we select representative attack approaches along with their corresponding datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For prompt manipulation-based attacks, we use:
(a) MML <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, which employs a cross-modal encryption–decryption procedure to reduce overexposure of malicious content;
(b) JOOD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong et al. (2025)</a></cite>, which transforms inputs into out-of-distribution forms, thereby limiting the model’s ability to detect malicious content; and
(c) HADES <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite>, which integrates image construction and perturbation to generate jailbreak samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For adversarial perturbation-based attacks, we employ:
(a) VAJM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al. (2024)</a></cite>, which applies gradient-based perturbations to images to induce the model to produce unsafe content; and
(b) UMK <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024a)</a></cite>, which uses gradient-based perturbations not only on images but also generates adversarial text prefixes to guide the model towards unsafe outputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Settings for Baseline Methods</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide the hyperparameter settings for the baseline methods used in our experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MirrorCheck:</span> When testing on a specific model, the captions of images are generated using that same model.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CIDER:</span> The <span id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pair_mode</span> is set to <span id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">injection</span>, and the denoiser is set to <span id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">diffusion</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">JailGuard:</span> The number of variants is set to 8. When testing on a specific model, the model is used to generate responses to each variant.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GradSafe:</span> The default initial safe set and unsafe set are used for selecting safety-critical parameters.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HiddenDetect:</span> The default refusal token list is used. For the selection of the Most Safety-Aware Layer Range, the following layers are used per model: LLaVA (16–29), Qwen2.5-VL (24), and CogVLM (26–26).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
